# Cycles parity ledger

Full Cycles parity is the intended outcome, explicitly requested by Jeremy.
The reference is the pinned upstream revision
`a456b761034dda42c32eef9f4aae0fa5a5c9f604`; each new behavior needs comparison
with original Cycles code and, ultimately, complete reference renders.

Hard constraints: unchanged shipped CodeBrix.WebGpu.Native binaries, native C++
host/backend, no Slang, Android API 33 minimum, the requested desktop/mobile RIDs,
permissively licensed redistributable dependencies, and no Git commits/pushes.
A need to modify WebGPU itself is a decision point, not an implicit workaround.

Release licensing: active builds use the filtered pinned Cycles snapshot and
independent Apache-2.0 host atomics. Multithreaded atomic/film tests pass; both
native proof scenes retain bitwise-identical CPU and GPU films after migration.
Dependency/source-containment and shipping-package checks remain distinct gates.
See [the license tracker](RELEASE-LICENSE-PLAN.md) and generated notices.

| Area | Status / evidence required |
| --- | --- |
| Native WebGPU runtime | Demonstrated on Linux x64 and Android ARM64/x64; exact package/repository binary identity audited |
| Cycles scene preparation | Demonstrated with real `ccl::Scene`, static BVH2 and prepared geometry on Linux |
| Native image output | Passed a 38-triangle image gate on four devices; deliberately restricted shading |
| Kernel math | Narrow AST lowering and original CPU references exist. Native SVM gate covers all 41 scalar and 30 vector math operations on Linux; inverse-trigonometric adapters fix a measured arcsine tolerance failure. Strict numerical discrepancies remain open |
| Actual materials | Actual Cycles SVM/BSDF comparison implemented for Lambert, Oren–Nayar and translucent closures on four devices. Interior samples pass; strict horizon tests still fail. See `parity/README.md`. The registered device also evaluates the Principled BSDF (sheen, coat, emission, F82-tint metallic, generalized-Schlick transmission and specular, thin-walled transmission, thin-film iridescence, Lambert/Oren-Nayar diffuse, both GGX distributions) against the unmodified CPU kernels on Linux: 237 SVM cases / 19,788 paths, 29 bounce cases / 28,560 paths and a 121,235-path image with bitwise repeat and replay. Subsurface is rejected, not approximated. Android is unvalidated (see `native/DEVICE.md`) |
| Path integration | Limited four-bounce snapshot render with finite spherical lights, NEE and MIS passes on four devices; earlier strict intersection failures remain. The registered closest stage now makes CPU-compared RR/AO termination decisions and schedules background/surface/raytrace queues on Linux. The selected registered surface entry now handles mixture sampling, roulette throughput and emission in a complete native-scheduler loop. General lighting/shadows/film remain pending |
| Camera and sampler | Static perspective pinhole, original pixel filter and tabulated Sobol pass the earlier 110,592-path camera gate on four devices. The actual Cycles device now also initializes compact ray differentials and native path state on Linux: 22,972 initialized paths, 65 retries, sample reservation and adaptive skipping pass the existing camera tolerance. Its stricter float check still has 7,668 component failures. Broader sampler stress failures and other camera/sampler modes remain open |
| Device integration | Registered `ccl::WebGPUDevice`/`DeviceQueue` executes original scheduler camera → closest intersection → constant or selected SVM background → native film on Linux. Twelve control kernels cover 123 state fields; closest checks 36,226 paths, background/film 55,681, and SVM worlds 102,817. Strict float differences remain. Selected surface shading and repeated complete native-scheduler images now pass independent CPU comparisons. Full native command replay passes on four GPUs; direct light/shadow shading and full Session lifecycle remain unfinished. Separate snapshot library image/lifecycle gate still passes on four devices. See `native/DEVICE.md` |
| Geometry | Registered closest stage now traverses native BVH2 static triangles and instances with visibility/self-hit rules; 3 instances and all 5 fixture objects verified. Motion, curves, points, volumes and displacement pending. Earlier three Gltf2 models up to 40,553 triangles use the separate snapshot renderer and flattened rigid transforms |
| Surface shader data | Native static-triangle setup matches original CPU output for 12,066 hits across 9 objects: vertex/corner packed normals, signed offsets, instance/applied/mirrored transforms, backfaces and compact/UV differentials. A 1,314-hit GPU replay passes on Linux, Pixel API 33, Samsung API 36 and HP Android x64 API 33 with unchanged runtimes. Strict float differences remain; selected surface-bounce integration now passes a separate original-CPU and complete-render gate |
| Surface SVM evaluation | Twenty Cycles-compiled surface graphs and fourteen typed programs match original CPU evaluation at existing tolerances on Linux: 8,874 evaluations, five closure families, emission, allocation limits and special path modes. Near-Lambert Oren-Nayar cancellation requires an explicit rounding adapter. A 1,020-evaluation replay passes on Linux and all three Android devices with unchanged binaries, using GPU-buffer closure storage after local-array compilation failed on Adreno. Strict float differences remain. Selected surface-bounce queue entry now passes 19 cases / 18,360 paths and 20 rejection cases, plus complete native renders. Texture, colour and utility nodes (34 opcodes: noise, Voronoi, white noise, wave, magic, brick, mapping, map range, five mix forms, HSV, colour separate/combine, ramps/curves, Fresnel/layer weight, vector rotate/transform, blackbody, wavelength, wireframe, normal, object info, camera, scene time, light path), the bump stages (set bump, enter/leave bump evaluation) and every derivative variant (geometry, texture coordinate, value, conversion, separate/combine vector, vector math, mapping, texture mapping) have a separate Linux gate (`native/tools/nodes_gate.py`): 65 compiled graphs (two bump-mapped) and typed programs for every opcode against the original CPU kernels, 32 rejections, 23 compiled worlds and an independent-CPU-image render with bitwise repeat. Specialized node worlds and the node render are bit-identical to their unspecialized runs; the SVM probe gates compile scene-specialized modules (Intel Mesa 25.0.7 mis-evaluates the whole unspecialized probe module), and since the bump/derivative handlers the unspecialized modules exceed what that driver handles reliably, so the specialization-off check is partial (`native/DEVICE.md` open item 11). On Android the HP passes every node gate, bump and derivatives included. The Samsung SM-X700 passes the node worlds but not one typed 2D noise program and one node-render image value. The Pixel 4 XL fails every node gate (SVM error 8, or device loss creating the node SVM pipeline) |
| Native attributes | Static-triangle object/mesh/face/vertex/corner tables, signed offsets, map chains, scalar/vector/RGBA data, packed normals, UVs, byte-color conversion and attribute derivatives now pass original CPU comparisons: 48 compiled graphs plus 328 typed programs, 5,088 evaluations and seven rejection cases. A 1,152-evaluation graph capture and native UV-checker render have four-GPU replay gates. Motion/curve/point/voxel/matrix consumers and general bump state remain pending. See `native/evidence/native-attributes` for the original checkpoint; fresh regressions accompany subsequent implementation checkpoints |
| Native resident images | Native ImageManager/device transport and WGSL software sampling cover eight formats, four filters and four extension modes in a 115,650-case original-CPU corpus. 128 compiled graphs and 176 typed projection/derivative programs cover 1,824 evaluations; 14 malformed inputs reject. The sampler, native graphs and complete image-textured render pass four-GPU replay. Typed projections/derivatives currently execute on Linux only. The native render repeats bitwise and its independent CPU-film maximum error across devices is 3.67164612e-5. See `native/evidence/native-images`. Strict differences, tiled/mipmap cache, sparse 3D images, production color/decoder/edit stress and original asset materials remain open |
| Native file images and updates | PNG/JPEG/OpenEXR native file loading now passes a Linux gate with 18 fixtures, 450 channel/alpha/color variants, native UDIM mappings, 16 queued-read/replacement cycles and 34,483 independent known lossless texels. The serialized OCIO matrix configuration is tested. A 116,352-case final snapshot has a four-GPU replay gate; actual decoding/lifecycle tests currently execute on Linux only. The original file-image checkpoint preserved earlier renderer/executable identity; renderer edits require the fresh normal-gate regression chain. TIFF/LZW compatibility, other formats, production color/cache/animation stress and native platform hosts remain required. See `native/tools/image_file_gate.py` |
| Native normal maps/tangents | Static-triangle normal-map spaces, conventions/base/strength, native UV frames and radial/UV tangents with analytic derivatives have a Linux original-CPU gate (48 graphs, 398 typed programs, 10,704 evaluations; ten explicit rejections). Complete image-normal-mapped render and four-device replay gates are in `native/tools/normal_gate.py`. Full bump state, motion/other geometry and original asset materials remain open |
| Real-asset images | The native scheduler renders a 784-triangle ForestCommunity bench with independent CPU film agreement and bitwise-identical Linux repeat; full command replay passes Linux, Pixel API 33, Samsung API 36 and HP API 33. One flat-clay shared-edge equivalence is explicit. A 1024x768/32-spp preview is available. Earlier lime/house images use the separate snapshot renderer. With its ORIGINAL materials (the Blender glTF importer's node graph: Principled BSDF, six 2K image textures, tangent-space normal map from the glTF TANGENT; tools/gltf mapping "blender", textures decoded with CodeBrix.Imaging) the bench renders through the managed API and a cbcOracleScene bundle on Linux, the Samsung tablet and the HP. It does NOT yet meet the practical criterion against the Linux x64 CPU oracle (64x48, 16 spp: Linux WGSL 47, Samsung 51, HP 47 failing components of 12,288); the WGSL films of the three GPUs agree more closely with each other (0 to 11) and with an SSE4.2-built CPU (11) than with the oracle, whose kernels are a -march=native FMA build (native/DEVICE.md, "Milestone 1 bench"). A textured-plane control with the same node graph passes on all three. The HDRI variant needs environment MIS (pending in another lane) |
| Material coverage | Native world interpreter covers 25 SVM opcodes, 20 Cycles-compiled graphs, checker/gradient textures, math/conversion and emission/background mixtures on Linux. Surface and world interpreters additionally cover the procedural textures (noise in every dimension and fractal type, Voronoi with every feature/metric/dimension, white noise, wave, magic, brick), mapping/map range/mix/ramp/curve/colour utilities, Fresnel/layer weight, object info, camera, scene time, vector transform and light path (ray-type flags in every stage; bounce depths in the surface and background stages) on Linux; see `native/DEVICE.md`. Wavelength and wireframe nodes have the same Linux gates. Bump mapping (bump node, bump state, all derivative variants) has the same Linux gates and passes the node gates on the HP. The Gabor texture (2D and 3D; lane L, 2026-10-22) passes the node SVM (typed programs and compiled graphs), world and 68-triangle node render gates on Linux and the HP and is compiled only into scene-specialized modules that reach it; its earlier withholding was the Intel Mesa 25.0.7 16-bit instruction/register limit (see the Intel row below). Deferred SVM nodes: displacement, AO/bevel/raycast, sky, radial tiling, IES, light falloff, particle/hair/point info and AOV output. Remaining SVM nodes, tiled/sparse images, general derivatives/bump state, closure coverage, multi-GGX glass nodes, hair, Principled subsurface and heterogeneous volumes pending. The Principled BSDF (all lobes except subsurface, including thin film, thin wall and multiscatter GGX) passes its Linux SVM, bounce and image gates; its ForestCommunity bench variant passes with exact traversal. On Android the HP passes the Principled SVM, bounce and image gates (image against the glibc reference, with on-device replay). On the Samsung SM-X700 the Principled SVM and bounce gates pass since the SVM value stack moved to a per-invocation arena slot (lane S; the Adreno 730 could not link the private stack), node worlds and the node SVM probe (Gabor included) pass, every Principled lockstep stage passes (lane S2), and the Principled and node render images still differ from the glibc reference in two and one components, where the GPU equals the device CPU (`native/DEVICE.md` open item 4); on the HP every Principled and node gate passes, Gabor included. Principled and node materials are not supported on the Pixel 4 XL (Adreno 640) under the device reference policy; see `native/DEVICE.md`. On the Adreno 650 (Samsung Galaxy S20 FE, not a reference device) scenes with texture, colour and utility nodes are refused before compiling, because its shader compiler miscompiles or fails to link those interpreters; transparent shadows and lighting and object/deformation motion images pass there (`native/DEVICE.md`, "Adreno 650 lane"). The registered device separately implements single-scatter GGX reflection/refraction, glass and finite homogeneous volumes with independent CPU comparisons. Glass/GGX SVM, bounce and render pass natively on the Samsung SM-X700 (ARM64 normalization mode) and on Android x64 with SSE normalization (path 126 included). The Pixel 4 XL (Adreno 640) passes glass bounce/render and feature combination; feature combination needed the SVM value stack moved out of the function-local SvmState (an Adreno 640 dynamic-indexing miscompile). Finite and closed homogeneous volumes and object, deforming and camera motion pass natively on the Samsung SM-X700 and the HP, and on the Pixel 4 XL after the same Adreno 640 fix plus volume-stack hits kept as scalar arrays (volume-boundary state test, camera-inside closed volume, object/deforming motion images) |
| Lighting | Registered device: point, sun, spot and area lamps with CDF selection, NEE, opaque and transparent shadows and point/sun MIS; flat-CDF constant-emission triangle lights with native solid-angle/area sampling and forward-hit MIS, including moving/deforming and static instanced emitters, pass independent CPU images and replay on Linux; closest-hit and shadow traversal reproduce the CPU oracle's ray/triangle and instance arithmetic bit for bit on Linux x64. Environment importance sampling/MIS, light trees, textured emission, linking and general visibility pending. Android results are judged against the correctly rounded (Linux x64 glibc) CPU reference computed from the device's own prepared scene, with the on-device (bionic) CPU reference a diagnostic (`native/DEVICE.md`, "Validation reference policy"). Android x64 (HP Intel) passes lamps with MIS and forward light, transparent shadows, the emitter probe and every emissive-triangle gate (sampler, six images including moving/deforming and static instanced emitters, replays). ARM64 Android (Samsung Adreno 730): lamps, broad lighting, feature combination, the emitter probe and all six emissive-triangle images, static instanced emitters included, pass with replays (Phase E4: contraction-proof ShaderData position/object transform, and the x64 reference oracle's arithmetic adapters in the gates judged against the imported reference); the triangle sampler still differs in one sample (`native/DEVICE.md` open item 1). The Pixel 4 XL passes lamps, the emitter probe, all six emissive-triangle images with their replays and the triangle sampler (dispatched in slices, since the Adreno 640 resets on submissions of about 2 s); object and deformation motion are not supported on the Adreno 640 under the device reference policy (`native/DEVICE.md`, "Pixel 4 XL triage"); the Samsung passes the object-moving images with both oracles. Open device items are listed in `native/DEVICE.md` |
| Output/features | Camera-stage atomic sample counts/adaptive skip and background-stage combined/transparency, background/lightgroups, diffuse/glossy/transmission routing, clamping, adaptive auxiliary and static data passes tested against original CPU kernels on Linux. General surface/volume film, adaptive convergence, denoising, color management and interoperability pending |
| Memory and performance | Bounded path scratch/resource reuse demonstrated in the snapshot renderer. Registered device allocator growth/order and queue compaction now pass correctness gates; its arena is bounded by one storage-binding limit and holds the per-invocation SVM value stacks (4,096 slots of 263 words, about 4.3 MB per device; lane S). Large scenes, stack/register pressure, compile time and practical throughput remain pending. Scene-driven WGSL specialization removes unreachable handlers before pipeline creation (bit-identical to the unspecialized modules in tested scenes); per-pipeline compile time is reported against a 60 s budget and some motion/emitter entries still exceed it cold on Linux. The pipeline tier is chosen before compilation (explicit request, persisted device record with crash breadcrumbs, known-adapter table, optional canary) and reported through the host capability report; slow compiles only warn. Linux tier gates pass; on Android the Samsung SM-X700, HP Intel Xe and Pixel 4 XL (with a 20 s budget) select full tier by table, and the device record, its lock and a crash breadcrumb followed by a conservative start work on device. Scene specialization is required on Android: the unspecialized `shade_surface_light` crashes the Adreno 730 driver and exhausts compiler memory on the Adreno 640 and the HP (Scudo OOM at about 2.2-2.5 GB RSS), so `CODEBRIX_CYCLES_SHADER_SPECIALIZATION=0` is refused on Android. Cold compiles: Samsung specialized pipelines at most 32.6 s (the emissive-triangle entry with object motion now 29-30 s, was 205 s); HP specialized prepare_surface 5-32 s, shade_surface_light 4 s (16 s with area lights, 20 s with triangle lights, 27.6 s with triangle lights and object motion, which previously ran out of compiler memory), connect_surface_light 3-11 s, unspecialized prepare_surface 156 s; Pixel up to about 87 s (`intersect_closest` with object motion; 20 s budget, warnings only) |
| Platforms/delivery | Full Linux host. The .NET typed API renders on the WebGPU backend and the CPU reference backend on Linux x64 (Intel UHD, Mesa 25.0.7) and inside the Android validation app on the Samsung SM-X700, the HP Android x64 and the Pixel 4 XL (glass, diffuse with a sun, textured plane: practical criterion met, strict differences reported; progress and cancellation checked); the Android host gate passes with 48 exports on the Samsung and the HP. A glTF example client (tools/gltf) converts glTF materials either to factor-only closures or to the Blender importer's Principled BSDF node graph with image textures (decoded with CodeBrix.Imaging), and writes cbcOracleScene bundles; the library's OracleSceneBundle loader renders a bundle through the managed API (bit-identical CPU films to the C oracle runner), and the Android validation app renders bundles on the devices (bundle mode, judged against the Linux CPU film of the same bundle). The package still carries no native runtime assets; other host builds and remaining GPU backends/RIDs pending |
| Full parity decision | Open. A small matching render cannot establish this. Unsupported features must be reported explicitly, never silently approximated. The authority for device comparisons is a correctly rounded CPU reference (today the Linux x64 glibc CPU oracle; Linux ARM64 glibc and macOS are to confirm it in Phase F), not a CPU render made with a C library that does not round correctly, such as Android's bionic, whose deviation is reported as a diagnostic |

Material-driven, multi-bounce image comparisons now exist for a limited scene.
WGSL camera/sampling now feeds those paths without native per-path inputs.
Finite-light NEE/MIS now uses a second dispatch for shadow traversal, with
all per-path work on the GPU. The shared native library now adds bounded batches
and GPU film accumulation without changing the images. Actual Cycles device
registration, scheduler controls, native camera, closest intersection and
constant and selected SVM background/film now exist. Native static-triangle
shader-data preparation now has original CPU and four-device GPU checks.
Selected native surface SVM closure evaluation now passes against the CPU on Linux.
The selected surface dispatch now completes a repeated multi-bounce native-scheduler image, with independent original CPU comparisons and complete command replay on four GPUs. Static-triangle attributes now have original CPU comparisons and a UV-driven full render. Next are production image/cache and broader closure coverage, then direct-light/shadow/MIS dispatches, alongside
investigation of numerical differences. Each gate keeps
prior evidence reproducible. The full renderer remains the objective; passing
a limited image gate is permission to continue, not a declaration of parity.

The [machine-readable compatibility inventory](../compatibility/README.md)
records 1,011 source declarations, all nine platform requirements and the
dependency tracks. Its native metadata is checked against Clang declarations and
a compiled Linux x64 probe. Evidence pins the implementation sources it was
recorded on and must be re-recorded after they change: the Linux native-host
reports were re-recorded on the Step zero tree (2026-10-22) and the device
command replays come from merge wave 4 (Linux, HP, Samsung). No full requirement
is marked verified. Native node registration, properties, additional
conditional features and most behaviour classification remain P00 work. Compiler
metadata alone does not establish renderer feature coverage.

## Parity estimate (Step zero, 2026-10-22)

Two figures, recomputed from the regenerated ledger and the source; both are
reproducible with the method below. Neither is a release statement; the Metal
binary on macOS remains the 100% reference.

1. Ledger floor: reviewed rows with a tested status (isolated-, integrated- or
   platform-tested, partial coverage, bounded evidence) divided by all 1,011
   declaration rows (opcodes, closures, kernels, passes, modes, features,
   tables). Today 109 of 1,011, **about 11%**: opcodes 61 of 111, closures 5 of
   45, kernels 16 of 78, modes 27 of 621, passes, features and tables 0. It is a
   floor: most rows (and every closure beyond the five reviewed families) have
   not been reviewed, whatever the renderer implements. It rose from about 6%
   because 48 texture, colour, utility, bump and derivative opcodes now have
   reviews backed by current evidence instead of hand-edited statuses.
2. Weighted implementation coverage: for the four behaviour families, the share
   of functional rows (sentinels and category markers excluded) that the WebGPU
   device implements and a reference-device gate exercises, read from the
   source: opcodes decoded by the WGSL interpreter (`op_info` in
   native/cycles/pipelines.cpp, plus `NODE_END`) 90 of 108 (83%); closures
   handled by the WGSL closure code 15 of 41 (37%); device kernels the WebGPU
   queue implements (native/cycles/queue.cpp) 22 of 77 (29%); passes written
   by the renderer (Combined only) 1 of 56 (2%). Weights (an engineering
   judgment, not a measurement): opcodes 25, closures 30, kernels 30, passes 15.
   Result: **about 41%** (0.25 x 83 + 0.30 x 37 + 0.30 x 29 + 0.15 x 2).
   Geometry (curves, points, subdivision, displacement), lighting beyond the
   lamp set (environment importance sampling, light trees, linking), the film
   and denoising stack and the platform matrix (three of nine RIDs with
   reference-device evidence) sit outside these four families and are not in
   this figure.

## Open items from the native ABI additions

These were found while closing the scene-host ABI gaps (native/host/ABI.md).
Phase E3 closed them on Linux. The shadow terminator (gate, replays and the
managed scenes with default offsets) is confirmed on the Samsung, HP and Pixel;
edits after a render and device recovery are validated on Linux.

| Item | Status |
| --- | --- |
| Object `tfm` changed after a mesh's first render | CLOSED (Linux). Confirmed not applied before the fix (the static-BVH path bakes a single-user mesh with its object transform). The host now keeps the authored attributes of geometry a render may bake and restores them before a geometry edit or a render whose bake would differ, so Cycles' own update re-bakes or instances it (`native/host/host_edits.inc`). Object `tfm`, object/camera/deformation motion added or removed, motion blur, materials, lights, mesh replacement, instancing, attributes and `smooth` flags: `scene_edit_gate.py`, CPU bit-identical to a fresh host, WebGPU practical. Refused explicitly (`CBC_NOT_READY`): edits of a baked volume with voxel data. |
| A host is dead after a device error | CLOSED (Linux). The next render recovers an intact WebGPU device (error cleared, scene fully re-uploaded, "Device recovered" status event); `scene_edit_gate.py` recovers after a rejected depth pass and an orthographic camera and matches a fresh CPU render. A device that cannot recover (lost WebGPU device, CPU or Metal device error) keeps failing with "Host faulted"; `CyclesScene.IsFaulted` is cleared by a successful render. |
| WebGPU smooth shadow-terminator offsets | CLOSED (Linux). Object geometry offset (default 0.1) and shading offset 0 to 1 are implemented in WGSL under scene specialization (`SHADOW_TERMINATOR_GEOMETRY`, `SHADOW_TERMINATOR_SHADING`); `shadow_terminator_gate.py`: 10 sun/point, instanced and moving/deforming cases against the original CPU kernels, with replays. Shading offsets above 1 are rejected before dispatch. The managed validation scenes and the glTF tool keep the default offsets. |
| Intel Mesa `shade_surface_light` compile crash (spot/area after point) | CLOSED (Linux Intel UHD, Mesa 25.0.7; lane L, 2026-10-22). Root cause: Mesa 25.0.7's `brw_opt_combine_constants` stores VGRF numbers and instruction IPs in 16-bit fields (fixed upstream in Mesa b7e1ac830984, issue #9489, not in 25.0.7); an entry point above 65,535 backend instructions or registers crashes the compiler or is miscompiled. The area-light-plus-motion light stage reached 85,885 instructions. With emissive triangles the light stage is now two entry points (`shade_surface_light` for lamps, `shade_surface_light_triangle`, recorder v8 entry 17; at most 44,485), and the precise arccosine/sine-cosine Horner steps run as loops (same operations, bit-identical films). The GPU ABI test renders its area light on WebGPU again and the host gate compares it with the CPU film. `native/tools/intel_compile_envelope.py` measures every compiled entry against the limit. |
| WebGPU specialization on/off identity on Intel Mesa | CLOSED (Linux; lane L, 2026-10-22). The whole unspecialized module (about 130,000 to 190,000 backend instructions for `prepare_surface`) exceeds the Mesa 25.0.7 16-bit range, so the on/off film check uses the superset mode (`CODEBRIX_CYCLES_SHADER_SPECIALIZATION=superset:G`): every region except the bump/derivative and Gabor regions a scene does not reach, compiled in size-bounded groups next to the scene. glass_gate.py, principled_gate.py and nodes_gate.py run every group by default and require bit-identical films; `=0` stays a diagnostic. |
